module BackwardPipeTrailingLineComment exposing (..)

-- A `--` ending the LAST body of a `<|` chain leaves the chain on its row.
--
-- `backwardMultiStep` sent the chain vertical when any body's box `endsOpen`,
-- and the clause's own reason is why that is off by one: "the ` <| ` of the step
-- AFTER it cannot share that row". Nothing is appended after the final body, so
-- its `--` swallows nothing.
--
-- Nothing else agreed with it. One step (`backwardSingleStep`) already had the
-- exemption as `singleLineSansTrailingComment`, and `|>`, `+` and `++` all keep
-- a trailing `--` on the flat row -- so a `<|` chain disagreed with its own
-- mirror operator, and with itself at a different length. elm-format keeps the
-- chain flat too; it evicts the comment to the top level, which gren's "a
-- comment never moves between rows" policy refuses (divergence #30), so the code
-- layout is now the same and only the comment's home differs.
--
-- Found by fuzzrun 2026-08-20 (seed 10145017), the one failure of that sweep's
-- 49 that was not the bracket-item bug.


twoStepsKeepTheirRow =
    Node <| 0 <| 1 -- k


threeStepsToo =
    Node <| 0 <| 1 <| 2 -- k


-- THE TWIN that always worked: one step goes through `backwardSingleStep`.


oneStepAlwaysDid =
    Node <| 0 -- k


-- THE MIRROR OPERATOR, which never broke.


forwardPipeTwin =
    0 |> f |> g -- k


-- THE BOUNDARY, and the reason the clause exists: a `--` ending a body that is
-- NOT the last one really would swallow the next step's operator, so that chain
-- still goes vertical.


midChainCommentStillBreaks =
    Node <|
        0 -- k
        <|
            1


-- The other always-breaking shape: a `--` glued AFTER the operator sits before
-- its own body, so there is always something after it to swallow.


gluedAfterOperatorStillBreaks =
    Node <| -- k
        0 <|
            1
